<div class="page">
  <div class="form-section">
    <h3 class="header-text">Instructions for Payer</h3>
    <p>Original issue discount (OID) is the excess of an obligation's stated redemption price at maturity over its issue price (acquisition price for a stripped bond or coupon). OID is generally taxable income to the holder of an OID obligation. If you hold an OID obligation, generally you must include an amount of OID in your gross income. The amount of OID you must include in your gross income is the amount of OID that is accrued during the year, not the amount of OID that is paid to you when the obligation matures. If you are a dealer in OID obligations, you may be able to elect to include the OID in your gross income only when the obligation matures. For more information, see Pub. 550, Investment Income and Expenses, for more information.</p>
    <p>If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income.</p>
    <p>If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income.</p>
    <p>If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income. If you receive an OID payment, you must report the amount of OID in your gross income.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="form-section">
    <h3 class="header-text">Instructions for Recipient</h3>
    <p>The recipient of an OID obligation must report the amount of OID in their gross income. The recipient must report the amount of OID in their gross income. The recipient must report the amount of OID in their gross income. The recipient must report the amount of OID in their gross income.</p>
    <p>For more information, see Pub. 550, Investment Income and Expenses, for more information.</p>
  </div>
</div>

